[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Failed-job incidents
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> After the final failed attempt, the runner sends an incident to `tracker` (Faultline).
[?2004l[?2004h> Successful runs do not send incidents. The failed oplog entry records `incident` as
[?2004l[?2004h> `sent`, `suppressed`, or `undelivered`; tracker outages do not change the job's exit code.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> `var/incidents.json` keeps per-fingerprint occurrence counts, the first failure time, and
[?2004l[?2004h> last successful send time (`null` until delivered). Repeats within
[?2004l[?2004h> `incident.dedupe_window_minutes` of the last successful send are counted but suppressed.
[?2004l[?2004h> The window uses the run's `--now` clock, not wall time. Failed deliveries count too, but
[?2004l[?2004h> never advance the last successful send time.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Incidents include the final attempt's last `incident.log_tail_lines` lines and the job's
[?2004l[?2004h> settings. Settings outside the tracker's accepted schema classes are masked; the ledger
[?2004l[?2004h> DSN is projected to its scheme/host/port/database, without credentials or query parameters,
[?2004l[?2004h> so on-call can still identify the replica. Restricted setting values (including Risk notes
[?2004l[?2004h> and encoded/decoded ledger passwords) are also scrubbed from outbound diagnostics. This
[?2004l[?2004h> projection does not change the settings or logs used by the job itself.
